  "schoolyard_use_notes": "This tree has glossy, dark green leaves that are perfect for teaching about leaf texture and structure. The bright red berries it produces can be used to discuss plant reproduction and the role of fruit in seed dispersal. Its evergreen nature makes it a great example for lessons on different types of foliage and plant adaptation to the Californian climate. The clusters of small white flowers that bloom in spring attract various pollinators, providing a practical example of plant-pollinator interactions.",
